in @prism
in @meta
in @glass
in @mirror
in @mirror/docs
in @epistemologic
in @epistemologic/property

# @mirror/docs/spec — the spec species of @mirror/docs.
#
# Tick 46 / docs-ouroboros tick 2. First species of the @mirror/docs
# family-root (#90 candidate, landed tick 45 at commit `f21cc30`).
#
# === The recognition this species declares ===
#
# `docs/specs/` is a heterogeneous catalog of substrate-decl
# specifications. Two kinds coexist that the family-root's
# taxonomy_kind variant collapsed into `spec`:
#
# 1. **Evergreen specs**: substrate-decl specifications rewritten in
#    place (e.g., `mirror-spectral.md`, `kintsugi-ci-v0.1.md`,
#    `mosaic-as-type-system.md`, `mirror-ref-spec.md`). No date
#    convention; the file IS the current state.
#
# 2. **Per-recognition canonical specs**: dated snapshots tied to a
#    specific recognition number. They accumulate as the cascade
#    grows; each carries `recognition-NN-<topic>-canonical-spec.md`
#    or `recognition-NN-research-<DATE>.md` shape. After the cascade
#    closed at #89/#90, six files of this kind sit in `docs/specs/`
#    flat. The Reed-agent cleanup audit (S-4 finding) flagged
#    consolidation into `docs/specs/recognitions/` before the next
#    cascade.
#
# This species substrate-decl's the recognition-spec sub-kind
# explicitly. Tick 2 executes the consolidation move: six files
# (cascade-recognition-76-through-80-canonical-spec.md;
# recognition-{76,79}-research-2026-06-18.md;
# recognition-{81,82}-*-canonical-spec.md;
# string-theory-tower-research-2026-06-18.md) move from `docs/specs/`
# to `docs/specs/recognitions/`. Evergreen specs stay at the top of
# `docs/specs/`.
#
# === Why this species NOW ===
#
# Three cascading reasons (Reed-agent cleanup S-4 / S-2):
#
# 1. Sprawl risk: `docs/specs/` had 107 flat files at audit time; 3
#    new recognition-canonical-specs landed in the 5 days before
#    audit. Without subdir consolidation, `docs/specs/` becomes
#    unfindable at corpus growth.
#
# 2. Sub-kind discrimination: per `[[architecture-property-fracture-
#    bilateral]]`'s discipline, taxonomy_kind variants that hide
#    sub-kinds (evergreen vs dated) drift into stringly-typed-
#    enum-in-disguise. This species names the substrate-decl form of
#    the sub-kind discrimination.
#
# 3. Recognition-pattern continuity: every recognition (#76 onward)
#    has a canonical spec; the spec convention IS load-bearing for
#    the substrate's auditable history. The subdir makes the
#    convention substrate-fact.
#
# === The spec_kind variant ===
#
# Per Seam C4 closure pattern: typed substrate variant naming the
# two canonical sub-kinds of spec. Same-shape different-meaning
# forbidden per [[feedback-no-bare-types]].
#
# - evergreen_spec: substrate-decl specifications rewritten in
#   place; live at `docs/specs/<topic>.md`; dating_discipline =
#   evergreen
# - recognition_spec: dated snapshots tied to a recognition number;
#   live at `docs/specs/recognitions/recognition-NN-<topic>-
#   canonical-spec.md` OR `docs/specs/recognitions/recognition-NN-
#   research-<YYYY-MM-DD>.md`; dating_discipline =
#   date_suffixed_on_respec
type spec_kind =
  | evergreen_spec
  | recognition_spec

# === The recognition_id carrier ===
#
# Typed reference for a recognition number (#76, #79, #80, #82,
# #83, #84, #85, #86, #87, #88, #89, #90, ...). Per
# [[feedback-no-bare-types]]: typed reference, not bare nat.
# Forward-promised refinement: typed parametric recognition_id once
# the substrate's recognition-tower type lands.
#
# Identity contract: byte-equality on the underlying ref.
type recognition_id = ref

# === The spec_record carrier ===
#
# Typed record over (path, kind, recognition_id?, dating). The
# substrate-architectural object @mirror/docs/spec instances are
# records carrying spec metadata.
#
# For evergreen_spec: recognition_id is the substrate's empty/null
# ref (forward-promised: option<recognition_id> when option family
# parametric over substrate-altitude types lands).
#
# Identity contract: byte-equality on the quadruple.
type spec_record = {
  path:           ref,
  kind:           spec_kind,
  recognition_id: recognition_id,
  dating:         ref,
}

# === catalog action ===
#
# THE LOAD-BEARING ACTION at @mirror/docs/spec altitude. Given a
# spec file path, returns the typed spec_record carrying its
# sub-kind classification + (for recognition_spec) the recognition
# number it documents.
#
# Body discharges at the realisation boundary; the classification
# heuristic reads the filename pattern (`recognition-NN-*` →
# recognition_spec; else evergreen_spec).
catalog(path: ref) -> spec_record { \ }

# === canonical_subdir_for action ===
#
# Returns the canonical subdir for a spec_kind:
# - evergreen_spec → `docs/specs/`
# - recognition_spec → `docs/specs/recognitions/`
#
# The substrate's convention-over-configuration discipline at the
# spec altitude.
canonical_subdir_for(k: spec_kind) -> ref { \ }

# === relocate action (FIRST non-decorative consumer of
# spec_in_canonical_subdir) ===
#
# Moves a spec_record's file to its canonical_subdir. The substrate-
# pull-correct equivalent of "move recognition specs to recognitions/
# subdir."
#
# requires spec_in_canonical_subdir(s): the substrate forecloses
# moves that would put a spec in the wrong subdir. Without this
# requires clause, relocate would admit misclassified moves (the
# @magic tick-11 dead-letter pattern at the spec altitude). The
# bilateral predicate IS the substrate's move-correctness check.
#
# Body discharges via the realisation boundary's git mv (preserving
# content-addressed history).
relocate(s: spec_record) -> ref
requires spec_in_canonical_subdir(s)
{ \ }

# === spec_in_canonical_subdir predicate (bilateral) ===
#
# Bilateral verdict per recognition #37 (Pask agreement) AND the
# alignment-as-boundary-mathematics check (#57) at the spec
# altitude.
#
# Returns verdict that a spec_record's classification is substrate-
# architecturally consistent with the canonical subdir for its kind:
# - evergreen_spec records land at `docs/specs/<topic>.md`
# - recognition_spec records land at
#   `docs/specs/recognitions/recognition-NN-*.md`
#
# Per Seam B1 closure pattern: relocate (above) is the FIRST non-
# decorative consumer via `requires spec_in_canonical_subdir(s)`.
#
# Discharge: substrate-pull-correct subdir-classification check at
# the species boundary. Bounded → spec is in its canonical subdir
# (admit relocate); unbounded → spec violates the subdir convention
# (relocate refused; manual review required).
#
# Same shape as the bilateral predicates the prior families carry:
# - @mirror/docs: doc_well_classified (the parent family-root)
# - @mirror/ref: ref_query_compositional, interface_authored_by_human
# - @mirror/bench: monotone_non_increasing,
#   bench_overhead_below_floor
# - @loop: loop_well_founded
# - @mirror/store/crystal: crystal_well_formed
# - @mirror/docs/spec: spec_in_canonical_subdir (this one)
spec_in_canonical_subdir(s: spec_record) -> verdict { \ }

out @mirror/docs/spec
out spec_kind
out recognition_id
out spec_record
out catalog
out canonical_subdir_for
out relocate
out spec_in_canonical_subdir
